  "description": "S1 relevance-gate concept lexicon (D-S1-GATE-01). Each concept lists the normalized surface forms (lowercase; hyphens, underscores and slashes read as spaces; a trailing plural 's'/'es' is always accepted) that count as evidence for it. A topic phrase matching any form becomes that concept's facet. 'sense' names a concept's head word and the modifiers that keep its meaning, so the head word used with any other modifier ('error injection') is recorded as a different sense. 'min_language_names' lets a concept be evidenced by that many distinct names from 'language_names'. Bump 'version' on every edit: it is stored with each collection run. Gate v2 (S1 audit, 2026-10-10) adds four optional keys. 'benign_sense' lists a concept's 'technique_context' forms and 'threat_context' word stems: a text that uses the concept alongside a technique form and no threat stem uses it in a benign sense. 'distinct_neighbours' names concepts for a related but distinct topic: a text whose title names a neighbour and not this concept, and which mentions the neighbour at least as often, is adjacent. 'incidental_followers' are words that make a form name a tool ('multilingual BERT'), not the subject. 'component_followers' do the same for a generic component noun ('multilingual embeddings') unless one of the 'component_target_cues' word stems occurs within the four words before the form: then the component is what is attacked ('attacks on multilingual embedding models') and the form counts. 'query_forms' are the forms validated as retrieval synonyms for source queries; each is sent as a quoted phrase (2026-10-12.2: the prompt-injection forms replace the separate words 'prompt' AND 'injection', which matched 59 arXiv records against 28 for the phrases, with all 20 gate-passing records in both). Top-level 'future_work_markers' mark sentences whose evidence does not count.",
